Task1
- To generate 1000 ages using the distribution method we first
calculate the probability of each age in in the student group.
Then we calculate the probabilities by adding each
probability to the previous ones. The cumulative probabilities
create intervals between 0 and 1.
We then compare the random number with the cumulative probabilities
and select the age corresponding to the interval in which the random
number falls.
By repeating this process 1000 times we generate a sample of
ages that follows approximately the same age distribution as the original
student group.

Task3
- A Singleton object cannot be created from outside its class
using the "new" keyword because its constructor is declared as
private. A private constructor can only be accessed from inside
the class itself.
The class provides a public static method usually called "getInstance()",
which creates the object if it does not already exist and returns
the existing instance if it does.
For example, a simulation program can use a Singleton Clock so that all parts
of the program use the same simulation time.

Task 4
- Normal distribution: Used to describe human heights,
measurement errors and exam scores. Most of the values are
close to the average while very high and very low values are less common.

- Uniform distribution: Used when all possible outcomes have
an equal probability. For example, generating a random number
between 1 and 10.

- Exponential distribution: Used to describe waiting times between
events such as the time between incoming customer calls.

- Poisson distribution: Used to describe the number of events
occurring during a fixed period of time, such as the number of customers arriving at a store in one hour.

- Binomial distribution: Used to calculate the number of successes
in a fixed number of attempts such as the number of correct answers
in a quiz.